part.text:
We need to add sounds. The user wants to add sounds to the game. We have a game.js file. Must include audio assets. We don't have any audio files. We can use Web Audio API to synthesize simple beeps, maybe for collect cargo, asteroid hit, game over, thrust.

We'll add code to create Audio objects with source URLs maybe referencing free sounds from CDN (e.g., https://www.soundjay.com/button/sounds/button-16.mp3). However offline environment may not have internet. But typical approach is to create Audio context and generate basic tones.

Simpler: create Audio objects using data URIs (small beep) to avoid external files. Use base64 encoded wav for beep.

We'll add sound initialization: const thrustSound = new Audio('data:audio/wav;base64,...'); similarly for collect, explosion, gameover.

Add play triggers: When thrust (ArrowUp) maybe play looped thrust sound. For cargo collect play collectSound. For asteroid collision play explosionSound, also for game over.

Implement minimal.

Let's generate small beep data URI. Could use simple beep encoded as base64. Example: a short 0.1s beep wav base64 "UklGRiQAAABXQVZFZm10IBAAAAABAAEAgD4AAAB9AAACABAAZGF0YQAAAAA=" maybe. But need proper.

Simplify: use the Web Audio API to generate tones on the fly.

Define function playTone(freq, duration) using AudioContext.

Implement a global audioCtx = new (window.AudioContext||window.webkitAudioContext)();

function beep(freq, dur){ const osc=audioCtx.createOscillator(); const gain=audioCtx.createGain(); osc.frequency.value=freq; osc.connect(gain); gain.connect(audioCtx.destination); gain.gain.setValueAtTime(0.1, audioCtx.currentTime); osc.start(); osc.stop(audioCtx.currentTime+dur); }

Then call beep(440,0.1) for collect, beep(200,0.5) for explosion etc.

Also add thrust sound continuous maybe use an oscillator that starts on ArrowUp and stops on keyup.

Simplify: on ArrowUp keydown, start thrustOsc; on keyup stop.

Implement.

Let's edit game.js: add audio initialization near top after ctx.

Add variables:

let audioCtx = new (window.AudioContext||window.webkitAudioContext)();

let thrustOsc = null;

function startThrustSound(){ if(thrustOsc) return; thrustOsc = audioCtx.createOscillator(); const gain=audioCtx.createGain(); thrustOsc.frequency.value=100; gain.gain.setValueAtTime(0.02, audioCtx.currentTime); thrustOsc.connect(gain); gain.connect(audioCtx.destination); thrustOsc.start(); }

function stopThrustSound(){ if(thrustOsc){ thrustOsc.stop(); thrustOsc.disconnect(); thrustOsc=null; } }

function playBeep(freq, dur){ const osc=audioCtx.createOscillator(); const gain=audioCtx.createGain(); osc.frequency.value=freq; gain.gain.setValueAtTime(0.1, audioCtx.currentTime); osc.connect(gain); gain.connect(audioCtx.destination); osc.start(); osc.stop(audioCtx.currentTime+dur); }

Then modify key handling: on ArrowUp set ship.speed etc also startThrustSound(); on keyup if ArrowUp released stop.

We need to detect keyup for ArrowUp.

Add listener for keydown/up as already; modify.

Implementation: In keydown handler, set keys[e.key]=true; if(e.key==='ArrowUp') startThrustSound();

In keyup handler, set keys[e.key]=false; if(e.key==='ArrowUp') stopThrustSound();

Then cargo collection: after setting collected: playBeep(800,0.1);

Asteroid collision: playBeep(200,0.3); Also game over maybe.

Add at gameOver call: playBeep(100,0.5);

Edge: ensure audio context resumed on user interaction; best to call audioCtx.resume() on first interaction.

Add in keydown listener: audioCtx.resume();

Now implement edits.

